              %li
                %a{:href => "#topos_homotopy_via_path_spaces"} Topos homotopy via path spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Viewing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} toposes
            as generalized spaces,
            %strong natural homotopy
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphisms
            generalizing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy relation
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous maps
          %p
            This relation is “natural” in the metaphorical sense of being the most “basic” such relation and in the metonymical sense of being defined via
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformations
          %p
            The correspondance between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theories
            and (classifying)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck toposes
            then induces an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            between theories finer than
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            that affords to define “homotopy theoretical” concepts for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℱ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E},\mathcal{F}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F}]
              denote the class of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+category"} connected components
              of their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-category"} hom-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi GrTop
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrTop
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{F}
              are called
              %em naturally homotopic
              , denoted
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\sim g
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f]=[g]
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformations
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi …
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*\leftarrow\cdot\rightarrow\cdot \dots\cdot\leftarrow\cdot\rightarrow g^*
          %p
            %strong Remark.
            Under the identification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕋
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi 𝕋
                  %mo −
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\mathcal{E},Set(\mathbb{T}))\cong \mathbb{T}-Mod(\mathcal{E})
            natural homotopy corresponds to an equivalence relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            on
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            given by zigzags of model homomorphisms.
          %h2#properties_and_some_homotopic_concepts Properties and some homotopic concepts
          %p
            Using the Godement calculus of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformations
            an induction over the number of zigzags
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ←
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo →
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot\leftarrow\cdot\rightarrow\cdot
            shows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi f
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\sim f'
            implies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\circ f\circ k\sim h\circ f'\circ k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\sim f'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi g
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\sim g'
            implies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ g\sim f'\circ g'
          %p
            In particular, one can define a (partial) composition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f]\circ[g]
            on natural homotopy equivalence classes via the composition of the representatives
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f\circ g]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi GrTop
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Ho}(GrTop)
            with objects the Grothendieck toposes and morphisms the natural homotopy equivalence classes of geometric morphisms.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A geometric morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{F}
              is called a
              %em (natural) homotopy equivalence
              if there exists a geometric morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:\mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{E}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∼
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ g\sim id_{\mathcal{F}}
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∼
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\circ f\sim id_{\mathcal{E}}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              are said to be of the same
              = succeed "." do
                %em (natural) homotopy type
          %p
            Homotopy equivalences are closed under composition hence “being of the same homotopy type” is an equivalence relation on toposes. Toposes having the same homotopy type as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GrTop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrTop
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A Grothendieck topos
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{T})\;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
              it
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifies
              %em (naturally) contractible
              , if there exists a “constant” geometric morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c:Set(\mathbb{T})\to Set(\mathbb{T})
              i.e. one factoring through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              as
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo !
                      %mi Set
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{T})\overset{!}{\to}Set\overset{\bar{c}}{\to}Set(\mathbb{T})\;
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∼
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mrow
                          %mi Set
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝕋
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\sim id_{Set(\mathbb{T})}\;
          %p
            In other words, a topos is contractible if its identity map is homotopic to a constant map and a geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            is contractible iff its classifying topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕋
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{T})
            is.
          %p
            Notice incidentally, that this requires by definition the existence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos"} point
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{T})
            = succeed "-based" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "-model." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos#toposes+without+points"} toposes without points
            there exist (Boolean) geometric theories that are not contractible.
          %p The correspondance between geometric theories and Grothendieck toposes together with the correspondance between their models and geometric morphisms similarly affords the transposition of other homotopic concepts to the realm of geometric logic.
          %p
            Let us take a closer look at constant endomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{T})\;
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕋
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo !
                  %mi Set
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕋
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Set(\mathbb{T})\overset{!}{\to}Set\overset{\bar{c}}{\to}Set(\mathbb{T})\;.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{c}
            classifies a
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            classifies a model (which is unique up to isomorphism since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            classified by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            whence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            classifies a
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
          %p
            Now, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image+functor"} inverse image part
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta\;
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+sheaf"} constant sheaf functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            are traditionally called “constant” whence we can think of the contractability
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{Set(\mathbb{T})}\sim c
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            as expressing the “almost constancy” of the generic model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi M
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi M
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{M}_\mathbb{T}\sim \Delta(\bar{c}^*(\mathbf{M}_\mathbb{T}))
            i.e. as a structural similarity between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi M
                    %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{M}_\mathbb{T}
            and a constant
            = succeed "-model." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
          %h2#example Example
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{D}_\infty)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schanuel+topos"} Schanuel topos
            that classifies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+infinite+decidable+objects"}
              theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔻
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}_\infty
              of infinite decidable objects
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{D}_\infty
            the generic infinite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+object"} decidable object
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_joyalwraith Proposition (Joyal-Wraith)
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔻
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{D}_\infty)\;
              , respectively the theory
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔻
                        %mn ∞
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}_\infty\;
          %p
            %strong Proof.
          %p
            We argue on the logical side:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            classifies the theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            of standard successor algebras whose models are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number+object"} natural number objects
            (cf. the example section at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Borceux vol.3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural numbers
            in Set is.
          %p
            Accordingly, we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{c}
            as the map that classifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}=\bar{c}^*(\mathbf{D}_\infty)
            as a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}_\infty
            in Set. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mo =
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !=\Delta\dashv\Gamma
            classifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(\mathbb{N})
            as a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{D}_\infty)
            and the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{c}\circ !
            classifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(\mathbb{N})=\Delta(\bar{c}^*(\mathbf{D}_\infty))
            as a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}_\infty
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set(\mathbb{D})
          %p
            Whence it suffices to exhibit a zigzag of homomorphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo −
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}_\infty-Mod(Set(\mathbb{D}_\infty))
            between the natural numbers object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(\mathbb{N})
            and the generic model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{D}_\infty
            but the coproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(\mathbb{N})+\mathbf{D}_\infty
            is both infinite and decidable, hence in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔻
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo −
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔻
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}_\infty-Mod(Set(\mathbb{D}_\infty))\;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ←
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(\mathbb{N})\rightarrow \Delta(\mathbb{N})+\mathbf{D}_\infty\leftarrow \mathbf{D}_\infty
            yield the desired zigzag of homomorphisms.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %h2#topos_homotopy_via_path_spaces Topos homotopy via path spaces
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"} Sierpinski topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^2
            is a connected, locally connected and local topos. As a result of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} Artin gluing
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi id
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set\overset{id}{\to}Set
            it has two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos"} topos points
            (corresponding to the two points of the underlying
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+space"} Sierpinski space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+topos"} exponentiable
            and, given a topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^{Set^2}
            as a path space for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            with respect to the abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^2
          %p
            (Cf. Beke
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#B00"} 2000
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+model+homomorphisms"} theory of model homomorphisms
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"} Sierpinski topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+space"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+presheaf+type"} theory of presheaf type
          %h2#references References
          %p The concept originates with
          %ul
            %li#JW84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Wraith"} Gavin Wraith
                %em Eilenberg-Mac Lane Toposes and Cohomology
                , pp.117-131 in Cont. Math.
                %strong 30
                AMS 1984.
            %li#W83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Wraith"}> Gavin Wraith
                ,
                %em Toposes and simplicial sets: the cohomological connection
                , pp.281-290 in
                %em Category theoretic methods in geometry
                , no.
                %strong 35
                Aarhus Univ. Var. Publ. Ser. 1983.
          %p An overview of homotopy theory done in this “toposophical” context is
          %ul
            %li#B00
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tibor+Beke"}> Tibor Beke
              ,
              %em Homotopoi
              , ms. University of Massachusetts Lowell (2000). (
              %a{:href => "http://faculty.uml.edu/tbeke/topos.dvi"}> dvi
              )
          %p The following papers explore the topos of sheaves on the unit interval as abstract interval object
          %ul
            %li#MW86
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Wraith"} Gavin Wraith
                %em Connected locally connected toposes are path-connected
                , Trans. AMS
                %strong 295
                no.2 (1986) pp.849-859.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Path-lifting for Grothendieck toposes
                %strong 102
                no.2 (1988) 2424-248
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2045869"} doi:10.2307/2045869
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An addendum to “Path-lifting for Grothendieck toposes”
                %strong 125
                no.9 (1997) 2815-2818
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2162060"} doi:2162060
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            A proof of the homotopy equivalence of certain
            %em petit
            and
            %em gros
            toposes can be found on p.415f in
          %ul
            %li#MM94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              %em Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
              , Springer Heidelberg 1994.
          %p A good source for the classical cohomology theory of toposes is chapter 8 of
          %ul
            %li#J77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em Topos Theory
              , Academic Press New York (1977). (Also available as Dover Reprint, Mineola 2014)
